WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.488
The library is one policy
set, numbered once.

2
00:00:03.488 --> 00:00:08.133
Policies CA001 to CA017 are the
baseline that Book 02 builds

3
00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:12.687
during the migration, and they
keep their identifiers here.

4
00:00:12.687 --> 00:00:17.013
CA002 stays retired, so
that's sixteen policies.

5
00:00:17.013 --> 00:00:23.349
Book 04 adds CA018 to CA038, for risk,
privileged workstations, segmented

6
00:00:23.349 --> 00:00:30.637
applications and workload identities, plus
CA090, the permanent report-only probe.

7
00:00:30.637 --> 00:00:38.662
Numbers 001 to 089 are for the library and
090 to 099 for report-only instruments.

8
00:00:38.662 --> 00:00:43.501
Numbers are labels, not order, and
a retired number is never reused.

9
00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:46.665
The earlier library used a
different numbering scheme,

10
00:00:46.665 --> 00:00:50.175
and the chapter maps every one of
its policies to where it went.

11
00:00:50.175 --> 00:00:53.097
Every name follows one pattern: number,

12
00:00:53.097 --> 00:00:56.750
population, resource or
condition, then control.

13
00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:59.562
You can read a policy on
a call without opening it.

14
00:00:59.562 --> 00:01:03.181
On the right are the prerequisites,
and missing ones are the most

15
00:01:03.181 --> 00:01:06.937
common reason a report-only policy
shows failures that aren't real.

16
00:01:06.937 --> 00:01:08.763
The emergency access group.

17
00:01:08.763 --> 00:01:11.953
Persona groups, the two
big ones dynamic from

18
00:01:11.953 --> 00:01:15.401
extension attributes in
Book 03's attribute map.

19
00:01:15.401 --> 00:01:18.100
Access groups for restricted populations.

20
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.750
Three named locations.

21
00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:23.592
A custom authentication strength
that accepts FIDO2 keys from

22
00:01:23.592 --> 00:01:27.225
an approved AAGUID list, or
certificate-based authentication.

23
00:01:27.225 --> 00:01:31.744
It leaves out Windows Hello for Business
on purpose: for privileged accounts,

24
00:01:31.744 --> 00:01:36.475
a credential held in the device gaining
access doesn't count as a separate device.

25
00:01:36.475 --> 00:01:39.600
A device label in
extension attribute twelve.

26
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.175
And a custom security attribute that
tags each application's environment.

27
00:01:44.175 --> 00:01:46.200
Four conventions apply everywhere.

28
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.300
The emergency access
exclusion is implicit.

29
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:53.631
Every policy needs Entra ID
P1; risk policies need P2;

30
00:01:53.631 --> 00:01:58.163
workload identity policies
need Workload ID Premium.

31
00:01:58.163 --> 00:02:00.850
Every policy is created in report-only.

32
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:04.611
And none uses the approved
client app grant,

33
00:02:04.611 --> 00:02:07.920
which became read-only on June 30, 2026.
